WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.703
This video shows how voice position reports work in vNAAATS.

00:00:07.703 --> 00:00:12.695
Concorde always reports by voice, and so can any other flight that isn't ADS-B equipped.

00:00:12.695 --> 00:00:15.901
For those flights, you type in what the pilot tells you,

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:18.764
and the system uses it to keep its estimate right.

00:00:20.064 --> 00:00:21.538
For Concorde, it's already on.

00:00:21.538 --> 00:00:24.868
For any other flight, click the callsign to open the aircraft menu,

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.060
and press Voice reports.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.531
It changes to Voice reports on, and a Position reports button appears,

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:31.565
in the menu and in the flight plan panel.

00:00:31.565 --> 00:00:34.491
It is for that one flight, everyone on the display sees it,

00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:36.873
and it switches itself off after eighteen hours.

00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:39.477
Switching it on or off clears the times typed so far.

00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:43.597
The button opens a table with every fix on the route,

00:00:43.597 --> 00:00:46.204
and three times: calculated, estimate and actual.

00:00:46.204 --> 00:00:48.258
Type each report as the pilot gives it.

00:00:48.258 --> 00:00:51.129
Put the time they passed the fix in the actual column,

00:00:51.129 --> 00:00:54.264
and their estimate for the next fix in the estimate column.

00:00:54.264 --> 00:00:56.108
Press now to fill the current time.

00:00:56.108 --> 00:00:58.953
Typed times replace the calculated times on the strip.

00:01:00.253 --> 00:01:01.565
Now the EuroScope screen.

00:01:01.565 --> 00:01:03.821
A voice report doesn't freeze the aircraft.

00:01:03.821 --> 00:01:05.604
It corrects the system's estimate.

00:01:05.604 --> 00:01:10.116
If nobody is tracking the aircraft, its tag is green and moves with its real position.

00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:12.341
Once it is tracked, by you or anyone else,

00:01:12.341 --> 00:01:15.572
the tag follows a calculated position, in the normal colours.

00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:20.282
The last report anchors it, and the tag then moves along the direct line to the next fix,

00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:21.605
arriving at the estimate.

00:01:21.605 --> 00:01:24.514
When the pilot reports the next fix and you type it in,

00:01:24.514 --> 00:01:28.058
the tag is re-anchored, and the same thing happens on the next leg.

00:01:28.058 --> 00:01:31.835
The system can't see weather deviations, so it keeps to the direct path.

00:01:31.835 --> 00:01:34.615
And no conflict alerts are raised for these aircraft.

00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:40.438
If the estimate for the next fix passes by more than three minutes with no report,

00:01:40.438 --> 00:01:42.865
the tag holds at that fix and flashes white.

00:01:42.865 --> 00:01:46.564
The strip, the Position reports button and the table row flash too,

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:48.884
and the top bar shows a red overdue count.

00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:51.510
Call the pilot for their report, and type it in.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.329
If you track an aircraft that is already in the oceanic airspace,

00:01:56.329 --> 00:01:58.332
ignore the earlier fixes on the list.

00:01:58.332 --> 00:02:02.942
No reports are needed for fixes it has already passed, and they never show as overdue.

00:02:02.942 --> 00:02:05.247
Start from the first fix still ahead of it.

00:02:06.547 --> 00:02:07.130
To sum up.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.109
Green means nobody is tracking it.

00:02:09.109 --> 00:02:13.535
Once tracked, the tag follows the calculated position along the direct line.

00:02:13.535 --> 00:02:15.049
Each report re-anchors it.

00:02:15.049 --> 00:02:18.601
A late report holds the tag at the fix, and flashes it white.
